{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target presents a false dilemma by asserting that the pragmatic defense necessarily reduces scientific rationality to a game of survival 'without teleological direction' and severs the grip on verisimilitude. This misrepresents Popper's actual position in his later work (e.g., 'Conjectures and Refutations' and 'Objective Knowledge'), where he explicitly argues that preferring better-corroborated theories is rational precisely because we conjecture that these theories have higher verisimilitude, even if we cannot prove it or assign it a probability. The preference does not 'smuggle in induction' if framed as a critical choice to test the most promising (most informative and best-tested) conjecture; the 'cost' described is not a forced concession but a misunderstanding of how critical rationalism operates without inductive logic. The target fails to engage with Popper's argument that acting on the best-corroborated theory is the only rational way to pursue truth if one accepts fallibilism, meaning the pragmatic defense does not inherently strip science of its directional goal toward truth.","premise":"The target claims that accepting the pragmatic defense forces Popper to concede that corroboration offers 'no grip on verisimilitude,' thereby severing the link between severe testing and truth-likeness.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"83a0dd47fd3e57646b9409f5","quote":"Accepting the pragmatic defense saves Popper from self-contradiction regarding induction but forces the concession that corroboration offers no grip on verisimilitude (truth-likeness)."}],"successor_question":"If the preference for better-corroborated theories is based on the conjecture that they are closer to the truth (higher verisimilitude) rather than an inductive inference, how does this maintain a teleological direction for science without violating Popper's ban on induction?","target":"cfcd00ba54028b01557b31ed255c30ecdd1cd89c79afdfef35ddc69bfc398732"}]}